    "reply": "{\n  \"claim\": \"The recovery kernel's rewrite operation is not idempotent for cells that already contain a fenced JSON object followed by prose or another object; appending a new object moves the recovered object instead of leaving the original cell unchanged.\",\n  \"expected\": \"For a cell that is already a valid cell of the grammar (e.g. fence[ A ], fence[ S A ], fence[ A S ]), rewriting it by appending another object should leave the original cell unchanged, because the last object inside a code fence is still the original object and the appended object is outside the fence.\",\n  \"observed\": \"The executed result is 'moved' rather than 'unchanged' for inputs such as fence[ A ], fence[ S A ], and fence[ A S ] when a new object is appended after the cell. The recovered object changes from the original object to the appended object.\",\n  \"reproducer\": \"```\\n{\\\"a\\\": 1}\\n```\\n{\\\"b\\\": 2}\",\n  \"source_basis\": \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\",\n  \"execution_evidence\": \"The test campaign reports executed: 'moved' and after: '{\\\"b\\\":2}' for the input fence[ A ] with rewrite fence[ A ] B, where the expected result was 'unchanged'.\",\n  \"scope\": \"All recovery-kernel rewrites that append a new object after a cell already containing a fenced JSON object, including cells with prose before or after the object inside the fence.\",\n  \"uncertainty\": \"The campaign records do not include the full grammar definition, so the exact cell grammar and whether appended prose/objects outside the fence should be considered part of the same cell cannot be independently verified. The reported INVALID_INSTANTIATION cases for other rewrites suggest the grammar may not permit multiple top-level objects, but the 'moved' results for the listed cases are inconsistent with the documented last-object-inside-a-fence rule.\"\n}"
